[candidate · 0:35] I'll assume it's store-operating level since that's where most of a coffee chain's cost sits, and verify later.

Here's my structure. Profit equals revenue minus costs, and we've largely cleared revenue: flat prices, flat footprint, volume up ~5%, ticket up 10%. So the driver is cost.

On costs I'd split three ways. One, COGS — coffee beans, dairy, cups and packaging. Commodity prices, supplier terms, and waste. Note that if customers buy more items per visit, COGS rises with ticket but so does revenue, so that alone shouldn't crush margin unless input prices moved.

Two, store operating costs — labor (wage rates, hours per store, turnover and training), rent and occupancy, utilities.

Three, below-store costs — corporate SG&A, marketing, any new systems or debt.

