[candidate · 1:21] Without competitor pricing I'll assume the market has broadly taken price, since inflation hit everyone — so Brew & Bean is likely under-priced relative to peers.

Levers beyond a straight price increase:

Pricing: targeted increases on the most inelastic items, espresso drinks, rather than across the board; regional pricing in higher-income markets; and raising the price of add-ons, since customers are already buying more items per visit.

Procurement: renegotiate or consolidate supplier contracts, forward-hedge or lock multi-year bean contracts, dual-source dairy and packaging, and buy in larger volumes across 200 stores.

Product and portion: reformulate drink specs, standardize portioning, cut the lowest-margin SKUs, right-size cups and lids, switch to cheaper packaging.

Mix: push customers toward high-margin items — drip coffee, pastries — via menu design and bundles.

Overhead: trim the two points of corporate creep.

One caution: a price increase is the only lever large enough to recover 18 points on its own.
