[candidate · 1:38] My recommendation: Brew & Bean should take price, phased, starting with a 10–12% increase now — the problem is not operations, it's two years of absorbing roughly 38% input inflation at flat menu prices.

The math: at current revenue indexed to 100, costs are 94 and profit is 6. Holding costs and volume constant, to get back to a 24% margin you'd need 94/(1+x) = 76, so prices up about 24%. That's too much at once and risks traffic. An 11% increase gets 94/1.11 ≈ 85, so roughly a 15% margin — a meaningful recovery without shocking customers.

First moves: a 60-day price test in 15–20 stores to get the elasticity we don't have, weighted toward espresso drinks and add-ons; in parallel, open supplier renegotiation and hedge bean contracts.

Risks: traffic loss if competitors held price, and continued inflation eroding the gain. Next step is competitor price benchmarking.
