[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:40] Understood. You named traffic loss and continued inflation as risks. Which single risk could most undermine this recommendation, and how would you detect it early and respond?

[candidate · 1:46] The one that could undermine it most is demand being more elastic than I've assumed — specifically, that the $6.80 ticket is being driven by a loyal but price-sensitive core, and an 11% increase pushes transactions down more than 11%, so revenue falls and the margin gain never materializes. Everything in my recommendation rests on elasticity I don't actually have data for.

How I'd detect it early: in the 15–20 store test, I'd track weekly transaction counts, items per transaction, and repeat-visit rate from the loyalty program against a matched control group — not just revenue, because revenue can look fine for a few weeks while traffic quietly erodes. I'd set a trigger: if transactions fall more than roughly 8% versus control by week four, we stop.

The response if it trips: narrow the increase to the least elastic items only, lean harder on procurement and mix shift toward high-margin drip and food, and accept a slower path back to a mid-teens margin.
